130 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 120 0 R
/T (A relational database organizes data into tables with a fixed schema defined in advance \(the CREATE TABLE statements of the airline design\). Data is normalized so each fact is stored once, and relationships are enforced through foreign keys. Transactions follow the ACID properties: Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation and Durability. Examples: MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle Database, Microsoft SQL Server.)
/E (A relational database organizes data into tables with a fixed schema defined in advance \(the CREATE TABLE statements of the airline design\). Data is normalized so each fact is stored once, and relationships are enforced through foreign keys. Transactions follow the ACID properties: Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation and Durability. Examples: MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle Database, Microsoft SQL Server.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
131 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 120 0 R
/T (Every application that stores data must choose a database model. For decades the relational database \(RDBMS\) was the default. It stores data in tables of rows and columns, links tables with primary and foreign keys, and is queried with SQL. Since the late 2000s, NoSQL \("not only SQL"\) databases have grown popular because web-scale applications needed to handle huge data volumes, flexible data shapes and many simultaneous users across many servers.)
/E (Every application that stores data must choose a database model. For decades the relational database \(RDBMS\) was the default. It stores data in tables of rows and columns, links tables with primary and foreign keys, and is queried with SQL. Since the late 2000s, NoSQL \("not only SQL"\) databases have grown popular because web-scale applications needed to handle huge data volumes, flexible data shapes and many simultaneous users across many servers.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
132 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 120 0 R
/T (This report compares the two families on scalability, consistency, performance and use cases, and explains why organizations pick different technologies. The running example is the Airline Reservation System designed in the earlier assignments: nine tables \(Passenger, Airport, Aircraft, Flight, Seat, Booking, Ticket, Payment, Baggage\) joined by foreign keys, with Booking resolving the many-to-many relationship between Passenger and Flight.)
/E (This report compares the two families on scalability, consistency, performance and use cases, and explains why organizations pick different technologies. The running example is the Airline Reservation System designed in the earlier assignments: nine tables \(Passenger, Airport, Aircraft, Flight, Seat, Booking, Ticket, Payment, Baggage\) joined by foreign keys, with Booking resolving the many-to-many relationship between Passenger and Flight.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [4]
>>
