141 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 121 0 R
/T (Relational: Traditionally scales vertically \(scale-up\): a bigger server with more CPU, memory and faster disks. This is simple but has a hardware and cost ceiling. Read load can be spread with read replicas, and large tables can be partitioned or sharded, but sharding a relational database is complex because joins and transactions across shards are hard. Distributed SQL systems \(for example Google Spanner, CockroachDB\) narrow this gap but are newer and more specialized.)
/E (Relational: Traditionally scales vertically \(scale-up\): a bigger server with more CPU, memory and faster disks. This is simple but has a hardware and cost ceiling. Read load can be spread with read replicas, and large tables can be partitioned or sharded, but sharding a relational database is complex because joins and transactions across shards are hard. Distributed SQL systems \(for example Google Spanner, CockroachDB\) narrow this gap but are newer and more specialized.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
142 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 121 0 R
/T (NoSQL: Designed to scale horizontally \(scale-out\): add more ordinary servers and the database spreads data across them automatically \(sharding\) and keeps copies \(replication\). Cassandra and DynamoDB are built around this idea, so capacity grows almost linearly with the number of nodes.)
/E (NoSQL: Designed to scale horizontally \(scale-out\): add more ordinary servers and the database spreads data across them automatically \(sharding\) and keeps copies \(replication\). Cassandra and DynamoDB are built around this idea, so capacity grows almost linearly with the number of nodes.)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
