/P 122 0 R
/T (Relational: Provides strong consistency through ACID transactions. After a transaction commits, every user sees the same data. Constraints \(PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE, NOT NULL, FOREIGN KEY, CHECK\) are enforced by the database itself.)
/E (Relational: Provides strong consistency through ACID transactions. After a transaction commits, every user sees the same data. Constraints \(PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE, NOT NULL, FOREIGN KEY, CHECK\) are enforced by the database itself.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
167 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 122 0 R
/T (NoSQL: Often offers eventual consistency. The CAP theorem states that when a network partition occurs, a distributed system must choose between consistency and availability. Many NoSQL systems choose availability, so a read may briefly return stale data. Many products let the designer tune this per query \(Cassandra consistency levels, DynamoDB strongly consistent reads\), and some, such as MongoDB, now support multi-document ACID transactions, though with more overhead than a traditional RDBMS.)
/E (NoSQL: Often offers eventual consistency. The CAP theorem states that when a network partition occurs, a distributed system must choose between consistency and availability. Many NoSQL systems choose availability, so a read may briefly return stale data. Many products let the designer tune this per query \(Cassandra consistency levels, DynamoDB strongly consistent reads\), and some, such as MongoDB, now support multi-document ACID transactions, though with more overhead than a traditional RDBMS.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
