/Title (Every application that stores data must choose a database model. For decades the relational database \(RDBMS\) was the default. It stores data in tables of rows and columns, links tables with primary and foreign keys, and is queried with SQL. Since the late 2000s, NoSQL \("not only SQL"\) databases have grown popular because web-scale applications needed to handle huge data volumes, flexible data shapes and many simultaneous users across many servers.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 21 0 R
/Next 289 0 R
>>
endobj
289 0 obj
<<
/Title (This report compares the two families on scalability, consistency, performance and use cases, and explains why organizations pick different technologies. The running example is the Airline Reservation System designed in the earlier assignments: nine tables \(Passenger, Airport, Aircraft, Flight, Seat, Booking, Ticket, Payment, Baggage\) joined by foreign keys, with Booking resolving the many-to-many relationship between Passenger and Flight.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 21 0 R
/Prev 288 0 R
>>
endobj
