/Title (A relational database organizes data into tables with a fixed schema defined in advance \(the CREATE TABLE statements of the airline design\). Data is normalized so each fact is stored once, and relationships are enforced through foreign keys. Transactions follow the ACID properties: Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation and Durability. Examples: MySQL, PostgreSQL, Oracle Database, Microsoft SQL Server.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 476 0 R
>>
endobj
513 0 obj
<<
/Title (NoSQL databases drop the fixed table model in favour of flexible structures. There are four main types:)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 477 0 R
/Next 554 0 R
>>
endobj
514 0 obj
<<
/Title (Many NoSQL systems favour the BASE model: Basically Available, Soft state, Eventually consistent. They accept that copies of data on different servers may briefly disagree in exchange for speed and availability.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 477 0 R
/Prev 555 0 R
>>
endobj
