556 0 obj
<<
/Title (Relational: Traditionally scales vertically \(scale-up\): a bigger server with more CPU, memory and faster disks. This is simple but has a hardware and cost ceiling. Read load can be spread with read replicas, and large tables can be partitioned or sharded, but sharding a relational database is complex because joins and transactions across shards are hard. Distributed SQL systems \(for example Google Spanner, CockroachDB\) narrow this gap but are newer and more specialized.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 515 0 R
/Next 557 0 R
>>
endobj
557 0 obj
<<
/Title (NoSQL: Designed to scale horizontally \(scale-out\): add more ordinary servers and the database spreads data across them automatically \(sharding\) and keeps copies \(replication\). Cassandra and DynamoDB are built around this idea, so capacity grows almost linearly with the number of nodes.)
/Dest [12 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 515 0 R
/Prev 556 0 R
>>
endobj
558 0 obj
<<
/Title (Relational: Provides strong consistency through ACID transactions. After a transaction commits, every user sees the same data. Constraints \(PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE, NOT NULL, FOREIGN KEY, CHECK\) are enforced by the database itself.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 516 0 R
/Next 559 0 R
>>
endobj
559 0 obj
<<
/Title (NoSQL: Often offers eventual consistency. The CAP theorem states that when a network partition occurs, a distributed system must choose between consistency and availability. Many NoSQL systems choose availability, so a read may briefly return stale data. Many products let the designer tune this per query \(Cassandra consistency levels, DynamoDB strongly consistent reads\), and some, such as MongoDB, now support multi-document ACID transactions, though with more overhead than a traditional RDBMS.)
/Dest [13 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 516 0 R
/Prev 558 0 R
>>
endobj
