602 0 obj
<<
/Title (Bookings, payments and tickets need a relational database. A seat on a flight can be sold only once. In the relational design this is protected by a transaction and a UNIQUE constraint on \(Flight_ID, Seat_ID\) for active bookings: if two customers try to buy seat 12A at the same moment, the database lets one commit and rejects the other. Foreign keys guarantee that every Payment and Ticket belongs to a real Booking, and a single transaction can create the booking, record the payment and issue the ticket together or not at all. With eventual consistency, two servers could each tell a customer that 12A is free, which creates the double booking problem in the analysis of the current schema. Flight search and caching suit NoSQL. Millions of travellers search flights far more often than they book. Search results can be cached in a key-value store such as Redis, so repeated searches answer in milliseconds without loading the main database. Short-lived data such as login sessions and a half-completed booking cart also fits key-value storage. Logs and activity data suit wide-column or document stores. Clickstream, search history, mobile app events and flight-status updates arrive in huge volume and are written once and read later. A system such as Cassandra can absorb these writes across many)
/Dest [14 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 561 0 R
/Prev 589 0 R
/Next 614 0 R
>>
endobj
